"""Livelihood restoration and just transition practice."""


def build(course, module):
    return course(99, 'Livelihood Restoration and Just Transition',
        'Livelihood Restoration\nand Just Transition',
        'Compare transition support against a livelihood baseline and design a monitored restoration pathway for affected workers and households.', [
        module('Map income, subsistence and care before change',
            'Construct a seasonal livelihood baseline that represents groups affected by an extractive transition.',
            """A livelihood combines resources, roles and relationships that let people meet ordinary needs and cope with shocks. Paid wages are one element. Subsistence production, household care, access to land and water, informal trade and mutual help can also sustain a household. Use a calendar to capture what people can do in the dry and wet seasons and when a household normally uses an asset. Compare patterns separately for employees, labour hire staff, suppliers, tenants and households that depend on affected shared resources. [S85]

Pay attention to household and individual differences. An aggregate household income entry may hide the loss of a woman's independent earnings or the way a longer water journey reduces time available for paid work. Record unpaid time separately and ask whose work changes. Include obligations such as a rotating savings payment, transport costs or seasonal debts when considering practical choices. Keep case identifiers to the minimum needed. In field practice, agree why details are needed and use authorised processes. [R98]

Build a comparison from the same periods and definitions. For a standard month, distinguish sales from net earnings after ordinary costs. Record which days, groups and costs were observed. If records are sparse, compare independently available statements about the same period and leave disputed figures labelled. The post-mining policy comparison across Ghana and two Australian jurisdictions identifies omissions that a narrow focus on land rehabilitation can miss. Use it to ask what a local baseline would need to show rather than importing its policy conclusions. [R96]

Finish with a coverage check. Describe a group not reached by the initial snapshot and the data source that could improve it. Identify a variable that may move independently of closure, such as seasonal prices, and explain how this limits a later claim about recovery.""",
            'A mine closure roster lists 60 employees and omits 15 regular food sellers. The first survey records revenue during the busy season.',
            'Draw a baseline table for employees, food sellers and unpaid carers. State one comparable cost or effort measure for each.',
            'Include reliance beyond signed employment, compare cost-adjusted figures and match the observation period to the proposed follow-up.',
            """
Map livelihood components. | What additional evidence helps when wage figures omit crops used for household meals? | Seasonal household production and access records. | The employer’s annual pay scale only. | A chart of shares sold. | A list of public holidays. | Subsistence contributes to livelihood and can change independently of wages.
Represent indirect workers. | Food sellers depend on workers buying meals during breaks. How should they enter the closure map? | As a distinct livelihood group with their own exposure and costs. | As the same employees on the payroll. | As people without any identifiable dependency. | As costs attributed only to rehabilitation machinery. | Dependence can extend beyond employment contracts.
Track unpaid time. | Water collection takes longer while household cash income stays steady. Which entry captures the change? | Specify additional unpaid time and who bears it. | Record no change in wellbeing because wages are stable. | Replace wage figures with an arbitrary money value. | Record the same time loss for all residents. | Time costs can reveal distribution hidden by cash totals.
Use comparable measures. | A vendor made 80 units in sales and paid 30 in ordinary costs. What is the relevant net amount for the recorded period? | 50 units with the period and costs stated. | 80 units after ordinary costs. | 110 units after adding ordinary costs. | 30 units recorded as total sales. | The net measure subtracts recorded costs from sales.
Check seasonal coverage. | Why would a baseline of only peak trading weeks be weak for comparing annual restoration? | It leaves lean periods and their coping needs unobserved. | Peak trading weeks include no useful evidence. | Annual recovery depends on employee wages alone. | Season can be corrected by changing the unit labels. | Comparable seasonal coverage prevents distorted conclusions.
"""),
        module('Design a transition with support people can use',
            'Compare transition options by access, work quality, timing and household viability.',
            """A just transition considers who benefits from a change in the economy and who bears the costs. The ILO guidelines connect skills, social dialogue, rights at work and social protection. For a local transition plan, begin with the timetable for job or access changes. A skills programme that starts after several months without income may leave people unable to participate. Use milestones that start early enough to connect people with support before their current options disappear. [S87]

Test any training offer against the jobs available, the accessibility of training and the likely earnings and conditions of the next work. Training completion is an intermediate step. For an employee with no transport, a practical route and shift schedule may be more important than another classroom place. For a supplier without capital, compare support to adapt an existing enterprise with entry into a different market. Compare options with the people who would use them, including contract staff, informal workers and people who paused work to provide care. [S87]

Evidence from mine closure policy documents across Ghana and Australia highlights potential gaps in workforce transition and community planning. That evidence helps frame questions about who has committed to do what. A signed policy still needs a budget, staff, eligibility criteria and a way for people to appeal. Record where the plan relies on an assumption about market demand, institutional approval or the household’s ability to carry a risk. [R96]

Write a support sequence that includes an accessible entry point, an authorised owner, a period for follow-up and an alternative if the first route fails. Show which costs happen immediately and which recur. Explain how workers or residents can influence a decision about changing the plan.""",
            'Thirty workers can take a certificate course, but only ten local roles appear in the case forecast and the first session overlaps with casual work.',
            'Compare training alone with one combined support route. Name a barrier, a work-quality measure and a contingency.',
            'Pair skills with realistic vacancies, accessible timing and appropriate transition support. Test employment quality and participation by excluded groups.',
            """
Sequence support. | Which transition timetable best protects people facing the end of wages? | Confirm accessible support and advice before income stops. | Publish the first contact after the final appeal window. | Schedule all training after an indefinite waiting period. | Measure uptake before invitations are sent. | Planning should reflect when workers actually lose access or income.
Test labour demand. | Thirty certificates are planned for ten expected jobs. Which question should be answered first? | What viable work or livelihood route exists for each participating group? | How many certificates fit on the page? | Which training room is largest? | Who can list training as employment immediately? | Credentials are an intermediate output; demand affects viability.
Check work quality. | Two placements pay the same amount, and one offers irregular hours with higher travel costs. What should reviewers compare? | Net earnings, stability, access and conditions. | Headline pay alone. | Number of certificates held. | Sponsor logo visibility. | Work quality and practical costs affect recovery.
Include labour hire workers. | A proposed support meeting invites direct employees only. What should the planner investigate? | Contract workers’ eligibility, barriers and representation. | How to add contract workers to the employee payroll without review. | How to remove seasonal conditions from the case. | What to do only after the meeting has concluded. | Different employment arrangements create different risks and support needs.
Check policy delivery. | A closure policy promises assistance but names no provider or funding. What would strengthen the proposal? | A costed plan with a named owner, scope and start date. | A printed copy of the policy alone. | A different uncosted promise. | A request to count planned places as delivered. | Budgets, owners and timelines turn a policy into an assessable commitment.
"""),
        module('Measure restoration over time',
            'Write indicators that compare recovery, inclusion and sustained access against a credible baseline.',
            """Livelihood restoration is assessed through outcomes over time. A settlement payment, course attendance or assistance offer can be important, yet a household may still lack access to land, reliable work or material support needed to sustain the new livelihood. Compare outcomes against a suitable measure from before the change. Test both the current position and how it varies across the seasons. A full comparison should explain what happened to those unable to use the support offered. [S85]

Define each measure and denominator before reviewing progress. A rate of twelve out of fifteen among attendees tells a different story from twelve out of thirty among eligible people. Use a consistent time period and record reasons for lost follow-up when available. Averages can improve while a small group remains affected, so review relevant groups separately where size and privacy permit. Clearly label protective suppression of small counts in public reporting. Keep a route to examine omitted groups through protected review. [S87]

When a change cannot be confidently attributed to support, describe the observed timing and plausible other factors. A rise in earnings may coincide with a strong harvest or an improvement in transport, while a lack of recovery may reflect constraints beyond the programme. Ghanaian research on livelihood and education pressures offers questions about how households adapt across domains. Use locally gathered, properly authorised evidence to determine whether education or care costs are changing in the case. [R98]

Make the response explicit when a group remains below its baseline. Specify who should review causes, which adjustment is feasible and when the team will check again. Document sustained use and affordability in the comparison before recommending that an assistance pathway close.""",
            'Twelve of 15 attendees reach the earnings target, from 30 eligible workers. Nine non-attendees report that transport is unaffordable.',
            'Calculate two rates. Write a protected reporting rule, an access adjustment and a sustained recovery check.',
            'Report 80 percent among attendees and 40 percent among eligible workers. Check who is missing and verify actual earnings after additional access support.',
            """
Measure sustained use. | Which finding best supports recovery after a one-off payment? | Comparable net earnings and access maintained over multiple relevant periods. | The payment was announced at a meeting. | The voucher was counted as used when printed. | The budget was spent in full. | A restoration outcome concerns the ongoing ability to sustain a livelihood.
Choose denominators. | Twelve of 30 eligible workers meet an earnings goal. What is the eligible-group proportion? | 40 percent. | 80 percent. | 12 percent. | 60 percent. | Divide twelve by the full eligible population of thirty.
Handle missing follow-up. | Several households cannot be reached for the recovery survey. Which reporting choice is defensible? | Report the missing observations and compare plausible coverage differences. | Count missing cases as recovered. | Assume all results are representative without qualification. | Treat each missing case as a new enrolment. | Missing observations can change interpretation.
Protect a small group. | Publishing a two-person breakdown would effectively identify both respondents. How should the team report it? | Use a protected review and a safe public aggregate. | Publish names to avoid ambiguity. | Remove the group from internal review too. | Create identical fictional entries labelled real. | Privacy protection and accountability can coexist.
Set a correction date. | A route adjustment increases attendance but earnings remain below baseline. What belongs next in the record? | Assign a review of remaining barriers and a scheduled earnings recheck. | Close the case because an activity target was met. | Replace earnings with attendance permanently. | Announce full restoration outside the observed cohort. | The result should drive a further action rather than a premature closure.
"""),
    ])
